      "body": "## Finding description\n\nWhen fulfilling a redeem request, there are no checks to ensure that $\\text{totalAssets()}$ is greater than the amount being fulfilled for the redemption. This omission will lead to an incorrect share calculation and to griefing (DoS) for users when they try to withdraw. I will describe the issue clearly below:\n\n\n```solidity\nfunction totalAssets() public view virtual returns (uint256) {\n        VaultStorage storage $ = _getVaultStorage();\n        uint256 balance = IERC20Metadata($.asset).balanceOf(address(this));\n        // Exclude pending deposits, pending/claimable cancelation deposits, and claimable withdrawals from total assets\n        uint256 reservedAssets = $.totalPendingDepositAssets + $.totalClaimableRedeemAssets + $.totalCancelDepositAssets;\n        return balance > reservedAssets ? balance - reservedAssets : 0;  <<--\n    }\n```\n\n\nIn the above function, if the current asset balance of this contract is less than the sum of $\\text{totalPendingDepositAssets}$ + $\\text{totalClaimableRedeemAssets}$ + $\\text{totalCancelDepositAssets}$, $\\text{totalAssets()}$ will return zero.\n\nLet's consider a case where User 1 has deposited $1\\_00\\_000$ assets. The user's deposit has gone through the three-step process ($\\text{requestDeposit} + \\text{fulfillDeposit} + \\text{deposit}$).\n\nNow let's say another user, User 2, has deposited $10\\_000$ assets. This user's deposit has also finished the three steps.\n\nNow the Investing Manager has invested $1\\_00\\_000$ assets in the settlement layer vault.\n\nThe current gloabl state is as follows:\n```bash\ntotalAssets=10_000 (1_00_000 invested in settlement layer)\ntotalPendingDepositAssets=0\ntotalClaimableRedeemAssets=0\ntotalCancelDepositAssets=0\ntotalClaimableRedeemShares=0\n```\n\nNow user1 want to withdraw their `1_00_000` assets and they will call `requestWithdraw`.\n\nNext, the Investing Manager fulfills the order.\n\n```solidity\nfunction fulfillRedeem(address controller, uint256 shares) public nonReentrant returns (uint256 assets) {\n        // ... logic ...\n -->    $.totalClaimableRedeemAssets += assets;  \n -->    $.totalClaimableRedeemShares += shares; // Track shares that will be burned\n        // ...\n        return assets;\n    }\n```\n\nOnce the request is fulfilled, it updates $\\text{totalClaimableRedeemAssets}$ and $\\text{totalClaimableRedeemShares}$. Now the state will be as below:\n\n```bash\ntotalAssets=10_000 (1_00_000 invested in settlement layer)\ntotalPendingDepositAssets=0\ntotalCancelDepositAssets=0\ntotalClaimableRedeemAssets=1_00_000\ntotalClaimableRedeemShares=1_00_000\n```\n\nNow, if we call $\\text{totalAssets()}$, it will return zero because the $\\text{current\\_balance}$ ($10\\_000$) minus the reserved amount ($\\text{totalClaimableRedeemAssets}$ of $1\\_00\\_000$) is less than zero. $\\text{totalClaimableRedeemShares}$ will return $1\\_00\\_000$ (referencing the updated state after $\\text{fulfillRedeemRequest}$, which is explained above).\n\n\nNow let's say a new user wants to deposit an additional $1\\_00\\_000$ tokens. In the $\\text{fulfillDeposit}$ logic, the contract will calculate shares based on the $\\text{convertNormalizedAssetsToShares}$ function from the $\\text{ShareTokenUpgradable}$ contract. The function logic is below:\n\n\n\n\n```solidity\nfunction convertNormalizedAssetsToShares(uint256 normalizedAssets, Math.Rounding rounding) external view returns (uint256 shares) {\n        // Get both values in a single call\n        (uint256 circulatingSupply, uint256 totalNormalizedAssets) = this.getCirculatingSupplyAndAssets();\n\n        // Add virtual amounts for inflation protection\n        circulatingSupply += VIRTUAL_SHARES;\n        totalNormalizedAssets += VIRTUAL_ASSETS;\n\n        // shares = normalizedAssets * circulatingSupply / totalNormalizedAssets\n        shares = Math.mulDiv(normalizedAssets, circulatingSupply, totalNormalizedAssets, rounding);\n    }\n```\n\n\n\nThe above function gets the current $\\text{totalShares}$ and $\\text{totalAssets}$ through a helper function and applies the normal share price calculation formula. The logic of the internal helper $\\text{getCirculatingSupplyAndAssets}$ function is below:\n\n\n```solidity\nfunction getCirculatingSupplyAndAssets() external view returns (uint256 circulatingSupply, uint256 totalNormalizedAssets) {\n        ShareTokenStorage storage $ = _getShareTokenStorage();\n        uint256 totalClaimableShares = 0;\n        uint256 length = $.assetToVault.length();\n\n        for (uint256 i = 0; i < length; i++) {\n            (, address vaultAddress) = $.assetToVault.at(i);\n\n            // Get both claimable shares and normalized assets in a single call for gas efficiency\n            (uint256 vaultClaimableShares, uint256 vaultNormalizedAssets) = IERC7575Vault(vaultAddress).getClaimableSharesAndNormalizedAssets();\n            totalClaimableShares += vaultClaimableShares;\n            totalNormalizedAssets += vaultNormalizedAssets;\n        }\n\n        // Add invested assets from the investment ShareToken (if configured)\n        totalNormalizedAssets += _calculateInvestmentAssets();\n\n        // Get total supply\n        uint256 supply = totalSupply();\n        // Calculate circulating supply: total supply minus vault-held shares for redemption claims\n        circulatingSupply = totalClaimableShares > supply ? 0 : supply - totalClaimableShares;\n    }\n```\n\n       \n                                      \n\nThe above function calculates the $\\text{totalClaimableShares}$ and $\\text{totalNormalizedAssets}$ by getting balances from each vault, respectively. For each vault, it calls $\\text{getClaimableSharesAndNormalizedAssets}$. The logic for $\\text{getClaimableSharesAndNormalizedAssets}$ is below:\n\n\n\n```solidity\nfunction getClaimableSharesAndNormalizedAssets() external view returns (uint256 totalClaimableShares, uint256 totalNormalizedAssets) {\n        VaultStorage storage $ = _getVaultStorage();\n        totalClaimableShares = $.totalClaimableRedeemShares;\n\n        uint256 vaultAssets = totalAssets();\n        // Use Math.mulDiv to prevent overflow for large amounts\n        totalNormalizedAssets = Math.mulDiv(vaultAssets, $.scalingFactor, 1);\n    }\n```\n\n\nAs stated earlier, in the above function, $\\text{totalAssets()}$ will return zero because $\\text{current\\_balance}$ ($10\\_000$) minus $\\text{totalClaimableRedeemAssets}$ ($1\\_00\\_000$) is less than zero. Accordingly, $\\text{totalClaimableShares}$ will return $1\\_00\\_000$ (referencing the updated state after $\\text{fulfillRedeemRequest}$, as explained above).\n\n\nNow $\\text{getCirculatingSupplyAndAssets}$ function will yield $\\text{circulatingSupply}$ and $\\text{totalNormalizedAssets}$ as:\n```bash\ncirculatingSupply = 10_000  ( `totalSupply (1_10_000) - totalClaimableShares (1_00_000) `)\ntotalNormalizedAssets = 1_00_000 ( `totalAssets (0)` + _calculateInvestmentAssets (1_00_000))\n```\n\nNow, since the new user is depositing $1\\_00\\_000$, the number of shares the user will get is calculated as: $\\mathbf{1\\_00\\_000 \\times 10\\_000 / 1\\_00\\_000}$, which is $\\mathbf{10\\_000}$ shares. This is incorrect, as the user should have received $1\\_00\\_000$ shares at the proper price. The magnitude of the loss depends on deposit amounts other than the fulfilled redemption. Since there is another $10\\_000$ deposit by User 2, the circulating supply is $10\\_000$; if there were no other users, it would be zero.\nNow, if User 1 tries to withdraw, User 1 can successfully withdraw $1\\_00\\_000$ tokens because their share price was calculated before the miscalculation occurred.\n\nIn the same way, if User 1 tries to withdraw before someone deposits 1_00_000, User 1's transaction will revert due to insufficient funds.\n\n## Impact\n\nThis will lead to share price manipulation and griefing ($\\text{DoS}$) for users.\n\n## Mitigation\n1. Add a check in $\\text{fulfillRedeem}$ to ensure that $\\text{totalAssets()}$ is greater than or equal to the amount being fulfilled for the redemption.\n\n```diff\ncontract ERC7575VaultUpgradeable{\n    .\n    .\n    .\n    function fulfillDeposit(address controller, uint256 assets) public nonReentrant returns (uint256 shares) {\n+       if(totalAssets()< assets) revert(\"Insufficient Funds: Try After Withdrawing Funds From Vault\");\n        VaultStorage storage $ = _getVaultStorage();\n        if (msg.sender != $.investmentManager) revert OnlyInvestmentManager();\n        uint256 pendingAssets = $.pendingDepositAssets[controller];\n        if (assets > pendingAssets) {\n            revert ERC20InsufficientBalance(address(this), pendingAssets, assets);\n        }\n\n        shares = _convertToShares(assets, Math.Rounding.Floor);\n        if (shares == 0) revert ZeroShares();\n\n        $.pendingDepositAssets[controller] -= assets;\n        $.totalPendingDepositAssets -= assets;\n        $.claimableDepositShares[controller] += shares;\n        $.claimableDepositAssets[controller] += assets; // Store asset amount for precise claiming\n\n        // Mint shares to this vault (will be transferred to user on claim)\n        ShareTokenUpgradeable($.shareToken).mint(address(this), shares);\n\n        return shares;\n    }\n    .\n    .\n\n}\n```\n\n**Note:** I have just given mitigation for `fulfillDeposit` function. You need to fix in `fulfillDeposits` function as well.",
